-- X20 (SHADOW-SEMANTICS S1-PRELUDE (b)) -- x19's member-RENAME twin; since #3708 the same value.
--
-- Both recovery spellings reach the prelude's method past the module's own `eq`.
--
--   PINNED: `(False, True)` -- the first component is the module's own `eq`, the second is
--     the prelude's `Eq` method reached through the rename `eqM`.
--   x19's `(False, True)` is the same program with `import core as C` / `C.eq` in place of
--     the rename.
--
-- WHY the rename escapes: an import member `x as y` binds the local name `y` and nothing
-- else, so `eqM` is the import's method and a bare `eq` is whatever this module's scope
-- says it is, which here is the standalone.  The member alias keeps a distinct spelling
-- through inference and is restored to its origin name after it
-- (`compiler/types/typecheck.mdk`, `resolveAliasMethodSpellings`), the way a module alias is.
-- VALUES HAND-DERIVED FROM THE RULE ABOVE, NOT CAPTURED.
import core.{eq as eqM}

eq : Int -> Int -> Bool
eq _ _ = False

main = println (eq 1 1, eqM 1 1)
